## 3. How Continuous Operation Changes the Role

The Symphony architecture defines the operational model: "Watch → Bootstrap → Execute → Supervise → Close → Learn. Each completed work item enriches the ORBIT context layer, making the next agent smarter. The system improves with every task — no retraining required." [Atlassian Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809/Atlassian+Symphony+AI+Employee+Orchestration+Platform)

### 3.1 24/7 Experiment Monitoring and Intelligent Early Stopping

**How continuous operation changes the role:**
- Experiments are not "submitted and checked tomorrow" — they are monitored at millisecond granularity
- Early stopping is based on formal convergence criteria, not "I checked and it looked flat"
- Training runs that show signs of instability (gradient explosion, loss oscillation) are caught and addressed within seconds, not hours
- The Digital MLE Vision specifically states the agent "monitors signal freshness against SLA; detects feature drift and training/serving skew" autonomously

### 3.2 Immediate Response to Data Drift Alerts

**How continuous operation changes the role:**
- The Digital MLE Vision: "Data refresh policy drift — the current model (trained2026-04-13) is already outside the 30-day refresh window. A digital MLE enforces the cadence automatically."
- No drift alert sits unacknowledged — the AI MLE is never asleep, never in a meeting, never on vacation
- Response to drift is not "triage → assign → investigate → fix" — it is "detect → diagnose → propose remediation → execute (within policy)" in a single continuous action

### 3.3 Real-Time Model Retraining Triggers

The DRAFT Continuous Training document describes the target: "a module can be written to calculate the metrics, adding to a table a list of tenants that have drifted. The training module takes in the table, and trains the model for each item." [DRAFT Continuous Training and Deployment in MLP](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/4409994937/DRAFT+Continuous+Training+and+Deployment+in+MLP)

**How continuous operation changes the role:**
- Retraining is triggered by real-time signals, not calendar cadences
- The AI MLE doesn't wait for "the next scheduled retraining window" — it evaluates continuously whether current model performance justifies immediate retraining
- Retraining decisions are based on the complete picture: current drift magnitude, compute cost, downstream impact, and confidence in the retrained model — all assessed simultaneously

### 3.4 No Handoff Gaps Between Shifts

**How continuous operation changes the role:**
- No "let me catch up on what happened overnight" — the AI MLE was there the entire time
- No information loss at boundaries (shift changes, sprints, quarters, team transfers)
- Perfect institutional memory: every decision, every experiment result, every failed approach is part of active context for all future decisions
- The AI Employee Strategy emphasizes: "Each completed work item enriches the ORBIT context layer for that service, repo, or model. Future agents start smarter."

### 3.5 Perfect Institutional Memory Across All Past Experiments

**How continuous operation changes the role:**
- The MLE Agent Capability Evaluation notes the target of "L3 agency (fully autonomous, zero human intervention)" with "time per end-to-end model onboarding" dropping from "~3-5 days" to "~4hours, hands-off" [MLE Agent Capability Evaluation —2026-05-06](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202437cfa6229b4f95a58ffcf2c483eb04/pages/6983354773/MLE+Agent+Capability+Evaluation+2026-05-06)
- No experiment is ever "forgotten" — if approach X was tried6 months ago and failed for reason Y, that knowledge is always present when considering approach X' today
- Pattern recognition across experiments is perfect: if the last 5 times a particular architecture was tried it failed under condition Z, the AI MLE surfaces this before the 6th attempt

