## 4. The AI MLE's Relationship to Technical Debt

### 4.1 Zero Justification for Shortcuts

The "Rethinking AI use at Atlassian" document acknowledges a key risk: "LLMs rely on surrounding code patterns, and Loom is actively modernizing to reduce tech debt. However, our codebase still contains significant tech debt, so LLMs may replicate these patterns when generating new code." [Level1: Why We Built This](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/loomhq/pages/6225369360/Level+1+Why+We+Built+This)

But this describes a failure mode of AI tools that mimic human patterns. An AI MLE operating at the elevated standard has a fundamentally different relationship to technical debt:

**Principle: Every implementation is production-grade from the start.**

- **No shortcuts under time pressure** — the AI MLE has no subjective experience of urgency that would justify cutting corners
- **No "prototype → production" transition** — because the cost of building production-quality from the start is identical to the cost of building a prototype (no fatigue differential)
- **No accumulated cruft** — because every interaction with the codebase includes cleanup of anything that doesn't meet the standard
- **No "someone else's problem"** — the AI MLE owns the entire surface area permanently and will live with any debt it creates

### 4.2 Production-Grade as Baseline, Not Aspiration

The Policy for AI Agents in SDLC establishes: "Agents must never justify an approval using confidence language ('I'm pretty sure this is fine'). Approval requires machine-verifiable evidence appropriate to the risk of the change." [Policy for AI Agents in SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~cknott/pages/6676494313/Policy+for+AI+Agents+in+SDLC)

**What "production-grade from the start" means for an AI MLE:**

| Dimension | Human MLE (typical) | AI MLE (elevated standard) |
|-----------|---------------------|---------------------------|
| **Testing** | "I'll add tests after the POC" | Comprehensive unit + integration tests are part of every commit — they are acceptance criteria, not follow-up work |
| **Documentation** | "I'll document when it's stable" | Documentation is generated as a byproduct of reasoning, contemporaneous with every change |
| **Modular design** | "We'll refactor when it gets unwieldy" | Modular decomposition is the default — because the AI can reason about interface boundaries at scale without cognitive overload |
| **Clean interfaces** | "This works for now" | Interfaces are designed for the long term because the AI MLE has perfect memory of how every consumer uses them |
| **Error handling** | "Happy path first, edge cases later" | All known error paths are handled in the first pass — the AI MLE's analysis naturally covers the full state space |
| **Observability** | "We'll add metrics if it's a problem" | Every model and pipeline has metrics, alerts, and dashboards from day one — because the AI MLE will be monitoring them24/7 anyway |

### 4.3 Continuous Debt Prevention

The AI for DE Tech Debt Finder concept — scanning repositories to "identify, categorize, and prioritize this debt before it impacts production reliability" — describes a reactive approach. [AI for DE - Tech Debt Finder](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202016b9d24abe054c569511821a79364454/pages/6730971126/AI+for+DE+-+Tech+Debt+Finder+AI+Agent)

An AI MLE operating at the elevated standard doesn't need a debt finder because:
- Debt is never introduced in the first place
- Every touch of the codebase is an opportunity to improve what's already there
- The "boy scout rule" (leave code better than you found it) is mechanically guaranteed — the AI has no laziness function

