## 5. Technical Domain Expertise Requirements

### 5.1 NLP & LLM Specialization (Primary Domain)
- **Transformer architectures**: GPT, BERT, T5, LLaMA, Qwen, Mistral, Mixtral
- **Fine-tuning techniques**: SFT (Supervised Fine-Tuning), RLHF, DPO (Direct Preference Optimization)
- **Parameter-efficient methods**: LoRA, QLoRA, AdaLoRA, adapters, prompt tuning
- **Inference optimization**: KV-cache management, paged attention, speculative decoding
- **RAG architecture**: Retrieval-augmented generation, embedding models, vector search
- **Prompt engineering**: System prompts, few-shot learning, chain-of-thought

### 5.2 Search & Ranking (Core Use Case)
- **Learning-to-Rank**: LambdaMART, neural rankers, multitask L2 ranking
- **Embedding models**: Two-tower architectures, BERT-based encoders, cross-encoders
- **Relevance metrics**: NDCG, MAP, MRR, CLIP (for interleaving experiments)
- **Online experimentation**: A/B testing, interleaving flights, shadow deployments
- **Query understanding**: Intent classification, query rewriting, entity recognition

### 5.3 Recommendation Systems
- **Sequential models**: HSTU (Hierarchical Sequential Transformers), temporal encoding
- **Collaborative filtering**: User/item embeddings, matrix factorization
- **Wide & Deep**: Multi-task models combining memorization and generalization
- **Feature interactions**: Deep & Cross Networks (DCN), factorization machines
- **Evaluation**: Recall@K, Coverage, Diversity, user engagement metrics

### 5.4 Computer Vision & Multi-Modal
- **Image moderation**: Content classification, safety filtering
- **Multi-modal models**: Vision + text (multi-modal LLMs)
- **Speech/Audio**: Acoustic models, speech synthesis, audio-driven generation
- **Optimization**:ONNX runtime, TensorRT compilation for vision models

### 5.5 ML Systems & Infrastructure Expertise
- **Model serving at scale**: Multi-model hosting, versioning, traffic splitting, canary deployments
- **Feature stores**: Online/offline consistency, training-serving skew prevention
- **Distributed systems**: Data-parallel and model-parallel training, checkpointing, gang scheduling
- **Performance optimization**: Latency profiling, GPU utilization optimization, batch size tuning
- **Reliability**: SLO definition, auto-scaling, circuit breakers, fail-open patterns
- **Cost optimization**: Right-sizing instances, mixed precision, quantization for inference efficiency

---

## 6. Key Patterns & Synthesis

### 6.1 The AI MLE Employee Should Master:
1. **End-to-end ownership**: Problem framing → data → modeling → deployment → monitoring → iteration
2. **Production engineering**: 70% software engineering + 30% ML (at minimum)
3. **MLOps maturity**: Move from Level 0 (manual) → Level 1+ (automated CI/CD/CT)
4. **Scale thinking**: Design for 50+ TB data, multi-region serving, sub-100ms latency
5. **Cost consciousness**: Balance model quality vs. compute cost (start8B + LoRA, scale when justified)

### 6.2 Critical Technical Differentiators:
- **Training-serving skew prevention** via unified feature stores
- **Continuous training** with automated quality gates and drift detection
- **Multi-cloud deployment** (AWS + GCP) with hardware flexibility (GPU/TPU)
- **Responsible AI**: Fairness, explainability, safety filtering, data governance compliance
- **Developer velocity**: Self-service workflows, reusable modules, standardized APIs

### 6.3 Architecture Principles:
- **Modular, composable ML pipelines** (atomic modules + YAML orchestration)
- **Docker-first** for environment isolation and cloud-agnosticism
- **Feature-as-code** for reproducibility and governance
- **Model optimization pipeline**: Train → Quantize → Benchmark → Deploy → Monitor
- **Separation of concerns**: ML Studio (training) → ML Registry (versioning) → TeamServe/MSP (serving) → ML Lens (observability)

