## 5. Common Challenges and Failure Modes

### Data Drift & Concept Drift
- **Data/Feature Drift**: When production data distributions diverge from training distributions, potentially degrading model performance. Detected via PSI (Population Stability Index), KS test (Kolmogorov-Smirnov), KL Divergence, and Wasserstein Distance. [Design ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349/Design+ML+Lens+Platform+Data+Drift+Detection)
- **Concept Drift**: Changes in the relationship between inputs and outputs (e.g., user preferences shift). Detected via reranking metrics (NDCG, MRR) degradation. [Growth Reco ML Observability Overview](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~82492541/pages/4424181036/Growth+Reco+ML+Observability+Overview)
- **Detection Approaches**: Platform-level drift detection computing metrics every ~5 minutes for all MLFS-registered features. Reference windows: training baseline (long-term structural drift), rolling 30-day (short-term operational drift), same-period prior year (seasonality-adjusted).
- **Current Gap**: "No pipeline currently implements formal data drift detection" in many teams — rudimentary day-over-day comparisons are common but insufficient.

### Training Instability & Reproducibility
- **Training run failures**: Managed via MLflow experiment tracking for reproducibility.
- **Model lineage**: Metadata registry tracks workflow runs, source data, configuration, enabling reproduction.
- **Version control challenges**: Rollback runbook example shows real-world complexity — model trained from one commit, deployed from another, with dependency bugs requiring multi-step tracing.

### Production-Research Gap
- **17Experiments to Production problem**: Atlassian documented significant "productionization debt" — successful experiments that lacked proper planning, scoping, capacity allocation to fully realize value. [17 Experiments to Production: Scaling Success](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-101017)
- **Handover protocol**: "The hand over protocol is not defined... typically contains artifacts. In other companies DS gives ML eng code that can produce the artifact."
- **CI/CD gaps**: Without CI/CD, "training runs happen manually, model promotion is a manual checklist, quality gates get skipped under pressure, deployments are ad-hoc." [CI/CD for MLOps](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6726837663/CI+CD+for+MLOps)

### Silent Model Degradation
- **Lack of monitoring**: ML models are probabilistic — unlike deterministic software, they can degrade silently. "ML systems can face feature drift, concept drift" without explicit error signals.
- **HOT-121489Incident**: L2 model performance degraded after scaling changes, with erroneous shadow call increases doubling traffic. "Inadequate pre-rollout checks failed to anticipate performance degradation." [HOT-121489](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/inckb/pages/5937013306/HOT-121489+LLM+Generated+PIR+using+Slack+data+and+Zoom+transcript)
- **Agent drift**: LLM agents degrade due to model updates, prompt drift, retrieval drift, third-party data drift, and infrastructure issues — requiring continuous health probes. [Agent Reliability, Regression & Drift Detection Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AA7/pages/6097247892/Agent+Reliability+Regression+Drift+Detection+Framework+for+FOR+LLM-BASED+AGENTS)

### Compute Cost Overruns
- **ML Studio FY26 costs**: ~$9.8M GPU (ML Fabric) + $7.5M Databricks jobs + ~$2M cross-region data transfer. [ML Studio Cost Optimization Opportunities](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120201e31a0a9605d4567baa8708c5fcb7237/pages/5673572367/ML+Studio+Cost+Optimization+Opportunities)
- **Common problems**: Over-provisioning clusters "just to be safe," under-optimizing job configuration, frequent retries, unnecessary long-running clusters.
- **Root causes**: "Engineers and MLEs have minimal visibility on their ML jobs cost efficiency" — hard to answer "what did this model training actually cost us?"

### Escalation Policies for Underperforming/Harmful Models
**Severity Matrix (CoreML):**
| Tier | Impact | Required Action |
|------|--------|----------------|
| P0 | High business impact, real-time outage | Acknowledge in2 hours; fix by EOD; if not → SEV3 HOT |
| P1 | High impact, weekly/bi-weekly inference | Acknowledge in 2 hours; fix by Day 2EOD; if not → SEV3 HOT |
| P2 | Moderate/Low impact | Acknowledge in 2 hours; fix in4 days; if not → SEV3 HOT |

Every model has an agreed SLA page for detection, response, and resolution. On-call MLE is first responder, escalating to Model Owner, then to formal HOT incident. [CoreML Incident Management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6052556269/CoreML+Incident+Management)

For harmful outputs:
- AI Safety filters block AUP violations immediately
- Automatic rollback when reliability score drops below threshold (PagerDuty/OpsGenie alerts)
- Disable experiment flag and revert prompt/model configuration
- Annual AI system re-evaluations for robustness and performance

